Yeah. Someone can have a dream in high fidelity in one dimension and barely have an outline in another. You might know exactly how you want a Saturday to feel without knowing what you’d do all day. Or you might be able to picture the house down to the porch lights and still be unsure what you wanted life inside it to feel like.

Take a dream you could call “Move Back to Richmond.” There are a few ways to render it:

* As a title: Move Back to Richmond.
* As a scene: Saturday morning. The kids are already outside with their cousins. Nobody had to plan it three weeks ahead.
* As a condition you can test: We see family often enough that being together feels ordinary.

Each version makes something clearer. The title gives you a place. The scene shows you what you’d actually miss if it never happened. The condition gives you a way to tell whether you got it.

And two people can both say “I want to move back” while privately holding different scenes. One is picturing the cousins in the yard. The other is picturing Sunday mornings with enough space to breathe. Before you compare plans, trade one scene each. You might discover that the dreams fit together, or that you’ve both been using the same title for different stories.